terature, shaping the narrative style and themes in the 19th century."}], "places": [{"name": "Paris, France", "role": "Major setting"}, {"name": "Montreuil-sur-Mer, France", "role": "Fantine's residence"}, {"name": "Arras, France", "role": "Jean Valjean's imprisonment"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Les Misérables", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "les-misérables"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q180736", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Les_Mis%C3%A9rables"], "tags": ["literary work", "Novels & Prose Fiction", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Les Misérables left a notable mark on Modern history, documented across 94 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "'Les Misérables' shaped literary and social discourse, influencing movements for justice and reform across generations.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-08T14:00:04Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27142557986", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "1862 novel by victor hugo Created by Victor Hugo. Created c. 1900.", "newValue": "Published in 1862, "Les Misérables" is a monumental novel by the French author Victor Hugo, which explores themes of justice, redemption, and the struggles of the underclass in post-revolutionary France. Set against the backdrop of the early 19th century, the narrative follows the lives of several characters, most notably Jean Valjean, an ex-convict striving for redemption, and Inspector Javert, who represents the law and its unyielding nature. The novel's profound social commentary and rich character development illuminate the harsh realities of poverty and the quest for dignity, making it a pivotal work in the canon of world literature.

